WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 120.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.300
It's July 3, 2026.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.680
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.780
A planet 25 light years away shows signs

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.040
it could support liquid water on its surface.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.160
The candidate planet orbits within the conservative habitable

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.700
zone of its host star.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.040
Radial velocity and transit data together

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.940
suggest a radius and insulation consistent

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.140
with possible surface liquid water.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.900
Follow-up spectroscopy could reveal

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.200
atmospheric composition in future observing seasons.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.800
The proximity allows relatively short integration times

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.560
compared with more distant targets.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.140
Additional transit observations are already scheduled

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.740
on ground-based facilities.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.420
From distant worlds to the vehicles that will one day

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.900
reach them, we turn to this week's major propulsion test.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.260
All six Raptor engines on the Starship Vehicle

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.680
fire together for a full minute at the star-based test site.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.380
The test clears the path for the 13th integrated flight

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.660
attempt and directly addresses questions

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.360
around rapid reuse cadence.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.100
Prepellant loading and engine performance data will now

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.120
be reviewed before stacking proceeds.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.680
This step follows the pattern of incremental hot fire

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.740
durations used in earlier vehicles.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.120
The next milestone watchers expect is roll out

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.020
to the launch mount.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.180
Remember the show covered Starship on Episode 118?

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.000
Today's test moves the rapid reuse question forward

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.960
with fresh engine performance numbers.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.140
While Starship pushes reusability forward,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.660
an older NASA telescope may soon get a commercial boost.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.200
The catalyst link robotic servicing spacecraft

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.920
now targets no earlier than July 3

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.920
for its attempt to raise the swift observatories orbit.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.860
The mission aims to extend operations

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.200
for the ultraviolet and x-ray telescope

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.680
that has already run more than two decades.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.440
Engineers completed final checks after the carrier plane

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.820
reached the drop point on July 3 without a confirmed release.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.020
Success would demonstrate commercial capability

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.540
to service aging astrophysics satellites.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.320
Teams will monitor telemetry immediately

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.860
after any launch attempt.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.600
NASA's update on the Swift Reboost

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.260
shows how commercial servicing can extend the life

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.740
of an observatory that has already outlived its original design.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.840
The link spacecraft must perform a precise rendezvous

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.740
and then apply a controlled thrust to raise the orbit

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.980
without damaging the telescope's instruments.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.420
Success would mark the first time

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.880
a privately developed vehicle has boosted

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.660
a NASA astrophysics mission.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.460
Engineers are watching propellant margins

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.840
and docking tolerances most closely.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.320
What remains unclear is whether similar boosts

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.700
can be offered as a routine service for other aging satellites.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.020
While regulators examine reentry effects,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.300
India's first private orbital rocket ready for flight.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.040
If every re-entering object the size of a small car

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.940
were stacked end-to-end, the line would stretch from Toronto to Montreal.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.260
Each one vaporizes at roughly 80 kilometers altitude,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.960
releasing a plume of metals and oxides

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.820
that spreads through the mesosphere and lower thermosphere.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.520
Those atoms can linger for months,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.460
changing the chemistry that normally destroys ozone

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.880
and altering the delicate balance of noctal lucent clouds.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.340
Laboratory chambers can reproduce the temperatures above 2000 degrees Celsius,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.900
yet they cannot duplicate the sheer volume of material

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.400
now entering every year.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.300
The atmospheric impacts of reentry and launch study

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.880
has now been formally published on Zenodo

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.500
after months of preparation.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.660
Researchers modeled how vaporized metals and particulates

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.140
from returning spacecraft alter upper atmosphere chemistry.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.400
The work provides the first comprehensive European data set

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.080
on cumulative launch and reentry emissions.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.740
Policy makers can use the findings to set future deorbit guidelines.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.040
The open question is whether this artificial layer

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.740
will eventually shift global circulation patterns

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.720
in ways current climate models do not yet capture.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.960
While regulators examine reentry effects,

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.240
India's first private orbital rocket ready for flight.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.800
India's first privately developed orbital rocket

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.360
now has a launch window opening July 12.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.920
The Vikram-1 vehicle will carry a technology demonstration payload

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.220
on its maiden flight.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:31.140
Success would mark the first Indian commercial orbital launch

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.380
and open a new domestic launch option.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.740
Ground crews are completing final vehicle integration

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.180
at the Satish Dauwan Space Center.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.200
Whether and range availability will determine the exact date

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.120
within the window.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.720
Another national agency is also ramping up after a quiet period.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.020
After six months with limited orbital activity,

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.540
the Indian Space Research Organization

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.220
is preparing a series of satellite deployments.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.800
The missions will restore launch cadence

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.320
and test new small satellite platforms.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.900
Payloads include earth observation

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.460
and technology demonstration spacecraft.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.960
Integration and testing at the launch site are reported to be on schedule.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.660
The upcoming flights will also validate

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.660
updated ground support procedures.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.460
While rockets rise, two famous telescopes offer complementary views

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:22.940
of a young star system.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.060
The James Webb Space Telescope captured an infrared view

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.120
of the FS Tau system that pierces thick dust

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.260
to show multiple bright protostars.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.660
Hubble's optical image reveals the same region

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.880
with the protostar FS Tau B.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.500
It's dusty disc and a cyan colored jet of outflowing material.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.040
Dust blocks most background stars and galaxies in the Hubble frame

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.860
while Webb reveals a richer tapestry of distant galaxies behind the nebula.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.200
The paired images illustrate how different wavelengths

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.400
expose distinct structures during the earliest stages of star formation.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.500
Follow-up observations will track changes in the jet over the coming months.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.000
From star formation to earth observation contracts,

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.220
Germany's Izar Aerospace wins new business.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.080
Germany's Izar Aerospace will launch a high-resolution imaging satellite

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.320
built by planets German subsidiary.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:22.120
The mission demonstrates an end-to-end national capability

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.980
from manufacturing through launch.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.820
Payload integration is scheduled at the German space port

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.020
once the rocket completes qualification.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.560
Data from the spacecraft will feed commercial earth observation services.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.560
This marks the first dedicated launch agreement

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:38.980
between the two entities.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.000
Another startup is adapting terrestrial solar tech for orbit.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.560
The startup is now developing thin film

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.920
Parovsky solar arrays specifically for large satellite constellations

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.240
and orbital data centers.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.020
Early rooftop terrestrial designs have been repurposed

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.260
for the vacuum and radiation environment.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.840
Laboratory tests show the cells maintain efficiency

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.920
after simulated launch vibration and thermal cycling.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.620
The company expects first flight hardware within two years.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.120
Partnerships with constellation operators are already under discussion.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.620
While hardware evolves, NASA's Artemis II broadcast set a new viewing record.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.580
Live streams of the Artemis II mission on NASA platforms

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.580
accumulated more than 149.4 million views.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.500
The continuous broadcast covered launch, lunar flyby,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.600
and splashdown without interruption.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.720
Sustained global interest remained high across every mission phase.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.120
The numbers exceed previous records for any single NASA event.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.080
Engineers are already studying viewer data

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.220
to refine future broadcast formats.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:49.500
Remember the show covered Artemis on episode 119.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.280
Today's streaming numbers move the outreach question forward

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.220
with concrete viewer totals.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.360
For a change of pace, European satellites offer striking views

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.640
of a familiar landmark.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.040
Two recent images from European satellites show the full length

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.920
of the Grand Canyon in Arizona.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.620
The views capture the depth of the Colorado River Gorge

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.660
and surrounding plateau topography.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:17.380
Data from the Sentinel series provide updated surface reflectance measurements

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.720
for geological mapping.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.120
The images also serve as calibration references

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.120
for future air-at-land studies.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.500
Additional acquisitions are planned

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.400
during the upcoming monsoon season.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.500
If you prefer the night sky,

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:32.860
the Galactic Center is well placed tonight.

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:36.540
The Galactic Center culminates above the southern horizon

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.740
shortly before midnight local time.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.920
Observers facing south under dark skies

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.440
will see the dense starfields of Sagittarius.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.280
Light pollution and horizon haze remain the main obstacles

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.420
for urban viewers.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:53.480
Binoculars or a small telescope reveal additional structure

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.920
along the Galactic plane.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.700
The same region will be observable again

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:00.080
in the pre-dawn hours later this month.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.260
Looking farther back in time,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.060
a new thermodynamic model revisits black hole entropy.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.680
A new thermodynamic framework suggests black holes

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.480
may obey Hawking-style laws

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.680
even when their horizons are changing.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.880
The model replaces the classical area law

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.700
with a quantity derived from horizon dynamics.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.180
Numerical simulations of a creating

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.900
and evaporating black holes

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.460
remain consistent with the revised entropy.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:29.700
Laboratory analogs using fluid systems

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.760
are being considered to test the idea.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:33.880
The work could resolve tensions

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.760
between quantum information and classical gravity.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:38.780
From theory to the samples

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.800
that still anchor our lunar knowledge,

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:42.860
Harrison Schmidt used a rake

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.680
to gather walnut-sized rocks

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.240
during the first Apollo 17 extravehicular activity.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.260
The samples provided some of the best preserved

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:53.360
highland material returned from the moon.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.460
Laboratory analysis later revealed the presence

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.000
of ancient crustal fragments.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.480
The rake technique remains a reference

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.820
for future robotic sampling systems.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:04.980
Archived footage of the collection

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.520
is now used in training for Artemis crews.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.700
Finally, a long-view essay asks

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.760
where all this infrastructure might lead centuries from now.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.860
A new essay examines

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.620
how American space capabilities

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.640
might evolve over the next 250 years.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:24.160
A contrast 1776 balloon flight

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.080
with current orbital infrastructure

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.600
and projects several plausible long-term trajectories.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.960
The piece emphasizes continuity

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.360
of national investment rather than any single technology.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.960
Policy and international cooperation

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:40.660
receive equal weight with hardware forecasts.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.360
Readers are invited to submit their own

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:44.900
century-scale scenarios.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.860
New data keep arriving from both the oldest

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:50.600
and newest spacecraft, plenty to keep watching.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.200
Keep an eye on the July 12 window

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.240
for Vikram 1 and any updates

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.980
from the Swift Reboost attempt.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.560
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

